BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the atmospheric oxidation chemistry of Sulfur Dioxide and Nitrogen Oxides generating Acid Rain, and detail its impact on marble structures.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Acid Rain Geochemistry, Atmospheric Oxidation Pathways, and Marble Cancer:) Tj
T*
(   1. Atmospheric Acidification Mechanisms:) Tj
T*
(   Combustion of fossil fuels emits gaseous sulfur dioxide \(SO2\) and nitric oxide \(NO\). In the) Tj
T*
(   troposphere, these gases are oxidized into strong mineral acids via homogeneous gas-phase and) Tj
T*
(   heterogeneous aqueous-phase reactions:) Tj
T*
(   - Sulfur Dioxide Oxidation Pathways:) Tj
T*
(   * Gas-Phase Photochemical Oxidation:) Tj
T*
(   SO2 + .OH -> .HSO3) Tj
T*
(   .HSO3 + O2 -> SO3 + .HO2) Tj
T*
(   SO3 + H2O -> H2SO4 \(Sulfuric acid aerosol\).) Tj
T*
(   * Aqueous-Phase Droplet Oxidation \(in cloud and rain droplets\):) Tj
T*
(   SO2 dissolves in water droplets: SO2 \(aq\) + H2O <-> HSO3- + H+.) Tj
T*
(   Dissolved hydrogen peroxide \(H2O2\) and ozone \(O3\) rapidly oxidize bisulfite:) Tj
T*
(   HSO3- + H2O2 + H+ -> SO4^2- + 2 H+ + H2O.) Tj
T*
(   This aqueous droplet mechanism accounts for > 70% of total atmospheric sulfate production.) Tj
T*
(   - Nitrogen Oxide Oxidation Pathways:) Tj
T*
(   * Daytime Gas-Phase: NO2 + .OH + M -> HNO3 \(Nitric acid vapor\) + M.) Tj
T*
(   * Nighttime Chemistry: NO2 + O3 -> NO3 + O2; NO3 + NO2 <-> N2O5; N2O5 + H2O \(aqueous\) -> 2) Tj
T*
(   HNO3.) Tj
T*
(   2. Acid Precipitation Thresholds:) Tj
T*
(   Unpolluted atmospheric CO2 generates an equilibrium pH of 5.6 via carbonic acid:) Tj
T*
(   CO2 + H2O <-> H2CO3 <-> H+ + HCO3-.) Tj
T*
(   Acid rain is defined as precipitation with pH < 5.6 \(frequently dropping to pH 3.8 to 4.2 in) Tj
T*
(   heavily industrialized regions\).) Tj
T*
(   3. Marble Cancer \(Degradation of Cultural Heritage / Taj Mahal\):) Tj
T*
(   - Historic monuments constructed of calcitic marble \(pure crystalline Calcium Carbonate, CaCO3\)) Tj
T*
(   are severely damaged by dry and wet deposition of sulfuric acid:) Tj
T*
ET

