(   2. Geochemical Origins of Fluoride Contamination:) Tj
T*
(   - Endemic across 19 Indian states \(Rajasthan, Gujarat, Andhra Pradesh, Telangana\).) Tj
T*
(   - Geogenic Dissolution: Weathering of fluorine-bearing igneous and metamorphic minerals) Tj
T*
(   \(Fluorite CaF2, Fluorapatite Ca5\(PO4\)3F, Cryolite Na3AlF6\) in granitic aquifers under alkaline,) Tj
T*
(   low-calcium groundwater conditions:) Tj
T*
(   CaF2 \(s\) + 2 NaHCO3 \(aq\) <-> CaCO3 \(s\) + 2 Na+ + 2 F- + H2O + CO2.) Tj
T*
(   3. The Nalgonda Defluoridation Technique \(NEERI, Nagpur\):) Tj
T*
(   - Engineering Protocol: Developed in 1974 by the National Environmental Engineering Research) Tj
T*
(   Institute \(NEERI\) for rural decentralized water defluoridation:) Tj
T*
(   - Sequence of Chemical Additions:) Tj
T*
(   * Step 1: Raw water containing excess fluoride is dosed with Lime [CaO or Ca\(OH\)2] to provide) Tj
T*
(   basic alkalinity and facilitate flocculation.) Tj
T*
(   * Step 2: Dosing with Alum [Aluminum sulfate, Al2\(SO4\)3.18H2O] under rapid mechanical flash) Tj
T*
(   mixing \(1-2 minutes\).) Tj
T*
(   * Step 3: Slow flocculation mixing \(10-15 minutes\), followed by gravity sedimentation \(1 hour\)) Tj
T*
(   and filtration through a sand bed.) Tj
T*
(   - Chemical Mechanism:) Tj
T*
(   Alum hydrolyzes into complex polyhydroxy-aluminum species and amorphous Al\(OH\)3 flocs:) Tj
T*
(   Al3+ + 3 H2O <-> Al\(OH\)3 \(am\) + 3 H+) Tj
T*
(   Fluoride ions \(F-\) possess an ionic radius \(1.33 A\) identical to the hydroxyl ion \(OH-, 1.33) Tj
T*
(   A\). In a rapid ligand exchange reaction, fluoride replaces hydroxyl groups in the aluminum) Tj
T*
(   hydroxide lattice or is co-precipitated:) Tj
T*
(   Al\(OH\)3 \(s\) + x F- -> Al\(OH\)_{3-x} F_x \(s\) + x OH-) Tj
T*
(   The dense, fluoride-laden aluminum flocs settle out, reducing fluoride from 10 mg/L down to) Tj
T*
(   safe drinking levels \(< 1.0 mg/L\).) Tj
