(   [Problem Statement]: Detail the biophysics of Ionizing Radiation \(Alpha, Beta, Gamma\), formulate Linear Energy Transfer \(LET\), and explain double-strand DNA damage.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Biophysics of Ionizing Radiation: LET, Relative Biological Effectiveness, and DNA Pathology:) Tj
T*
(   1. Physicochemical Typologies of Ionizing Radiation:) Tj
T*
(   Ionizing radiation carries sufficient energy \(> 10 to 12 eV\) to liberate orbital electrons from) Tj
T*
(   atomic nuclei, generating reactive ion pairs in biological tissue:) Tj
T*
(   - Alpha \(alpha\) Radiation: Heavy, high-energy Helium-4 nuclei \(2 protons, 2 neutrons, charge) Tj
T*
(   +2e, mass 4 u\). Emitted by heavy radioisotopes \(U-238, Ra-226, Pu-239\).) Tj
T*
(   * Extremely low penetration depth \(stopped by a single sheet of paper or the dead epidermal) Tj
T*
(   stratum corneum layer of human skin\).) Tj
T*
(   * Lethally hazardous if inhaled, ingested, or absorbed internally into living tissues!) Tj
T*
(   - Beta \(beta\) Radiation: Fast, relativistic electrons \(beta-\) or positrons \(beta+\) emitted) Tj
T*
(   during nuclear beta decay \(Sr-90, Cs-137, H-3\). Moderate penetration \(stopped by a few) Tj
T*
(   millimeters of aluminum or plastic\).) Tj
T*
(   - Gamma \(gamma\) Radiation & X-Rays: High-energy, short-wavelength electromagnetic photons) Tj
T*
